<% content_for :title, "Repositories · SpecGuard" %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "Repositories",
        subtitle: "Repositories you have registered, and those shared with you."
      ) do |header| %>
    <% header.with_actions do %>
      <%# Two entry points rather than one, because they are different jobs and not two sizes of the
          same one: registering the repository you came here for, and turning SpecGuard on across a
          whole GitHub account. Bulk is the secondary variant — most visits are the first job. %>
      <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :secondary, href: bulk_repositories_path) { "Register several" } %>
      <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :primary, href: new_repository_path) { "Register a repository" } %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# REGISTRATION ACCESS, on the page both return journeys land on.
      `SessionsController#post_authorization_path` and `GithubInstallationsController#destination`
      both fall back to `repositories_path`, so this is where somebody sent here by the `sgu_` API's
      `:not_granted` refusal arrives — and until now this page said nothing about GitHub connection
      state and offered no control that touches it.

      FOUR STORIES, NOT ONE. `RepositoriesController#registration_grant_story` keeps
      `GrantVerifier`'s verdict whole and then tells apart what that verdict is ABOUT, because a
      refusal and a landing page owe the reader different things. Over there the question is "may
      this request register?" and absent and stale answer it identically. Here they are different
      facts with different fixes, and the wording below is the reason the distinction is drawn at
      all — see that method.

      EVERY BRANCH BELOW ENDS IN A PICKER RENDER, because that is the sole place a grant is minted —
      so each one is only honest while the session holds a credential the picker can read GitHub
      with. `:session_expired` is the branch for when it does not, and it exists because without it
      the other two tell that reader to do something that cannot work: a blank token makes
      `Sources#complete?` false, `capture` returns nil, and the page redraws unchanged however many
      times its own instruction is followed.

      So the two branches below `:session_expired` may assume a live credential, and they say so
      plainly — nothing is broken for either of those readers, the browser path asks GitHub live and
      is unaffected, and what is refused is registration from an API key and nothing more. That
      sentence is TRUE THERE ONLY BECAUSE the credential-less reader was routed away first.

      Rendered ABOVE the grid rather than inside it, and outside the `@repositories.any?` branch on
      purpose: the reader most likely to be here is the one being refused by the API, and their
      account may hold no repositories at all. Gating it on the grid would hide it from exactly the
      person the refusal sent. %>
  <% if @registration_grant_story == :not_installed %>
    <%# NOTHING TO TAKE A SNAPSHOT OF — the App is installed on no account this user holds.
        Ordinary rather than exotic: it is every brand-new signup's first page, and it is also what
        CANCELLING OUT of GitHub's install picker leaves behind (`GithubInstallationsController#callback`
        records nothing in that case and says so in its own comment), plus anyone who installed from
        the App's listing and was bounced through `require_authentication` to sign in first.

        ⚠ THIS BRANCH EXISTS BECAUSE `:never_taken` ENDED IN A FALSE ALL-CLEAR FOR THIS READER, which
        is worse than the loop the branch below it was added to fix. Its copy promised that opening
        the picker would take a snapshot and that registering in the browser worked — both false
        here, since the picker offers this reader no repository at all. Worse, opening it MINTED an
        empty-but-fresh grant: `sources` answers a user with no installations with `installed: false`
        and NO error, so `complete?` is true and `capture` writes a row with `registrable: []`. The
        grant then stopped being stale, this whole panel disappeared, and the API went on refusing
        them — with `:not_in_installation`, which is not the state any branch here describes. The
        reader followed the instruction, the warning vanished, and nothing ever told them again.

        The story is read ABOVE the grant for exactly that reason (see `registration_grant_story`),
        so the empty row can never become a reason for silence.

        The control is `github_install_button` and NOT the reconnect: a credential reads an
        installation and this reader has none to read. Same existing helper, same existing route —
        no new route and no new button helper. It handles the unconfigured-App case itself, and the
        panel is gated on `configured?` besides. %>
    <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(
          tone: :info, title: "Connect the SpecGuard GitHub App to register repositories",
          class: "space-y-3"
        ) do %>
      <p>
        <%# The same slice of `InstallationRepositories::MESSAGES[:not_granted]` every branch here
            reads — one vocabulary across the API's refusal and this page. True of this reader in the
            plainest way: SpecGuard holds no record because there is no installation to read one
            from. %>
        <%= github_registration_lapsed_state %>
        The App is not installed on any of your GitHub accounts yet, so there is nothing for
        SpecGuard to check yet — connect it below and choose the repositories you want. Until then
        <%= tag.code("POST /api/v1/repositories") %> will refuse.
      </p>
      <div>
        <%# `return_to:` sends them to the picker rather than back here, on the same reasoning the
            branches below state: the picker is the sole site a grant is minted, so the journey ends
            where the state actually resolves. After the install it lists the repositories they just
            chose, and the grant is taken on that render. %>
        <%= github_install_button return_to: new_repository_path %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% elsif @registration_grant_story == :session_expired %>
    <%# NO CREDENTIAL TO READ WITH — the App is installed, and this session holds no usable token.
        The most ordinary state there is: a returning reader at the start of a new browser session,
        because a user-to-server token is short-lived and lives only in the session
        (`GithubUserSession`).

        THIS BRANCH EXISTS BECAUSE THE OTHER TWO LIE TO THIS READER. Both of them end by promising a
        picker render will take a snapshot. For this reader it cannot: `InstallationRepositories`
        answers a blank token with `:not_authorized`, so `Sources#complete?` is false and
        that capture method returns nil before writing anything. Following that
        instruction lands them on a picker that offers a reconnect instead of a repository, and
        returns them here to the identical panel — a loop, not a fix.

        So this says the one true thing and offers the one control that resolves it. No "again" and
        no expiry claim: the snapshot may never have existed, and the reader is not being told about
        the snapshot at all — they are being told about the credential, which is the nearer missing
        thing and the one blocking every route to the other. It also makes no promise about the
        browser path, because unlike the branches below it, the browser path is NOT unaffected for
        this reader — the picker will ask them for exactly this before it lists anything. %>
    <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(
          tone: :warning, title: "Reconnect GitHub to finish setting up API-key registration",
          class: "space-y-3"
        ) do %>
      <p>
        <%# The same slice of `InstallationRepositories::MESSAGES[:not_granted]` the other branches
            read, for the same reason: one vocabulary across the API's refusal and this page. It is
            the half true of every reader here — a statement about what SpecGuard HOLDS, which is
            equally true of a snapshot that expired and one that was never taken. %>
        <%= github_registration_lapsed_state %>
        Your browser session no longer holds permission for SpecGuard to ask GitHub for one, so
        reconnect below — SpecGuard takes a fresh record the moment you land back on the picker.
        Until then <%= tag.code("POST /api/v1/repositories") %> will refuse.
      </p>
      <div>
        <%# The EXISTING action, through the EXISTING helper — same as the `:lapsed` branch below,
            and `return_to:` is passed for the same load-bearing reason stated there: the picker is
            the sole place a grant is minted, so the journey must END there rather than back here.
            For this reader that destination does double duty — the reconnect restores the
            credential, and the picker it lands on then mints the grant with the credential it just
            got back. One journey, both missing things.

            Safe inside this alert for the reason the panel is gated on
            `SpecGuard::GithubApp.configured?` — see `registration_grant_story`. %>
        <%= github_authorize_button return_to: new_repository_path %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% elsif @registration_grant_story == :lapsed %>
    <%# AGED OUT — a snapshot was taken and has passed `GithubRegistrationGrant::MAX_AGE` (seven
        days). "again" is true of this reader and only of this reader: something was checked once,
        and it has expired. %>
    <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(
          tone: :warning, title: "SpecGuard needs to check your GitHub permissions again",
          class: "space-y-3"
        ) do %>
      <p>
        <%# Read from `InstallationRepositories::MESSAGES[:not_granted]` — the API's own refusal —
            so the sentence a reader meets here cannot drift from the one that sent them. See
            `github_registration_lapsed_state` for why it is a slice of that constant. %>
        <%= github_registration_lapsed_state %>
        Registering a repository with an API key needs one, so
        <%= tag.code("POST /api/v1/repositories") %> will refuse until it is taken again. Registering
        here in the browser is unaffected.
      </p>
      <div>
        <%# The EXISTING action — `github_installation_authorize`, through the existing
            `github_authorize_button`. No new route and no new helper; this is the same control the
            picker offers when a session has no credential, offered here for a different missing
            thing.

            ⚠ `return_to:` IS PASSED, AND IT IS THE HALF THAT MAKES THIS WORK. Left to default, the
            helper would send the reader back to `request.fullpath` — this page — where nothing
            would have changed. A grant is taken in exactly one place, and this page deliberately
            never touches that place, so returning here would hand back a credential the session
            already had, redraw this same banner, and offer this same button a second time.

            The picker is where the snapshot is actually retaken, at no cost and with no further
            click — so the button ends the journey THERE, and the state resolves itself on arrival.
            Still one capture call site; still zero GitHub calls added to this page.

            Safe to render inside this alert: the whole panel is gated on
            `SpecGuard::GithubApp.configured?`, which is the branch on which this helper would
            return `github_app_unconfigured_notice` — itself an alert — and nest one alert in
            another. See `registration_grant_story`. %>
        <%= github_authorize_button return_to: new_repository_path %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% elsif @registration_grant_story == :never_taken %>
    <%# NEVER TAKEN — the ordinary state of somebody who has just connected the App, whose very
        first page after the callback is this one. Nothing has lapsed, so this is `:info` and not a
        warning, and it says what is true: a step remains, not that something expired.

        NO RECONNECT BUTTON HERE, deliberately. This reader's session already holds a live token —
        sending them out to github.com and back would be a longer road to the same picker the
        header's "Register a repository" already reaches in one hop, and the grant is minted on that
        picker's render either way. Offering the external round trip would talk them out of the
        shorter fix. So this branch STATES the position and points at the control already on the
        page rather than adding a second one. %>
    <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(
          tone: :info, title: "One more step before API-key registration works"
        ) do %>
      <p>
        <%= github_registration_lapsed_state %>
        Open <%= link_to "Register a repository", new_repository_path, class: "underline" %> once and
        SpecGuard will take one, and registering here in the browser works now. Until then
        <%= tag.code("POST /api/v1/repositories") %> will refuse.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <% if @repositories.any? %>
    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3">
      <% @repositories.each do |repository| %>
        <%= render UI::CardComponent.new(href: repository_path(repository)) do %>
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%# The whole run, not a bare size — see RepositoriesController#latest_run. The badge
                below states the figure and the paragraph under the badge row states its basis;
                both read this one already-loaded row, and it is read here so the two cannot end
                up naming different runs. %>
            <% run = latest_run(repository) %>
            <%# Whether this repository's deliveries are being REFUSED — the verdict the connection
                indicator on `show` renders, which until now this grid had no access to at all. Read
                once here beside the run, on the same rule: both facts are read at the top so the
                badge row and the sentence under it cannot end up describing different states.

                The same `RejectedIngests` API `show` reads — `refusing?` and `last_rejection_at` —
                reached through RepositoriesController#rejection_verdict. The comparison is never
                re-spelled here; see that method and the model's class comment for why there is
                exactly one expression of the rule. Costs no query per card: the refused side is one
                grouped MAX for the whole page, the accepted side is the run already loaded above. %>
            <% rejections = rejection_verdict(repository) %>
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-2">
              <%= render UI::HeadingComponent.new(level: 3, class: "text-app-content min-w-0") { repository.github_full_name } %>
              <%# The list now mixes owned and shared repositories, and nothing else on the card
                  distinguishes them — which matters because `show` offers a destructive Remove to a
                  member holding `repo.delete`. The badge names the *owner account* — the person to
                  ask for a wider permission set — which is the same one `show` names in its
                  Overview panel. Not the slug's org segment, which names no SpecGuard account.
                  `user` is preloaded by the controller, so this asks nothing per card. %>
              <% unless owns_repository?(repository) %>
                <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :info, class: "shrink-0") { "Shared · #{repository.user.display_name}" } %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <%# The key count is credential information, and `show` gates every bit of it behind
                  `keys.manage` (see the API keys panel there). A count on this card would hand a
                  `view`-only member more than the page it links to is willing to. The suite size
                  below is not credential information — it is the same class of signal as the
                  connection-health stat on `show`, which is deliberately outside that gate — so it
                  stays for everyone.

                  The figure itself comes from RepositoriesController#api_key_count, grouped for
                  the whole page in one query — never a COUNT per card, the same rule the suite
                  size below is held to. The gate stays on what is *rendered*: the count is loaded
                  for every card, and read only where `key_count_visible?` says so. %>
              <% if key_count_visible?(repository) %>
                <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :neutral) { pluralize(api_key_count(repository), "key") } %>
              <% end %>
              <%# Suite size off the newest TestRun, preloaded for the whole page by
                  RepositoriesController#latest_test_runs — never a COUNT per card.

                  This replaces a `spec_intents` count that no production code path can make
                  non-zero: nothing on `main` writes that table, so a repository whose CI had
                  ingested forty-seven runs of a twelve-thousand-test suite still read `0 intents`
                  here. `total_specs_count` is derived by ingestion on every POST and counts the
                  whole suite, annotated or not.

                  `nil` means CI has never reported, and gets its own words rather than `0 tests`:
                  "never ingested" and "the suite is empty" are different facts and the card has to
                  be able to tell them apart. `show` draws the same line in its Overview panel. %>
              <%# `nil` run has TWO readings and they are opposite facts, which is the distinction
                  this branch exists to draw a second time. "No runs yet" is right for a repository
                  nobody has wired CI to. It is a falsehood for one whose CI is posting and being
                  REFUSED: deliveries are arriving, and the reason there is no run to show is that
                  every one of them is being thrown away. `RejectedIngests#refusing?` calls that
                  case "the most refusing state there is" — a rejection with no accepted run behind
                  it — and before this the grid drew it byte-identically to the never-wired card.

                  So the refusing variant says what is actually true of the row — no run was ever
                  ACCEPTED — in error tone, beside the marker above that says why. Same rule the
                  neutral wording itself follows: "never ingested" and "the suite is empty" are
                  different facts, and so are "nothing has been sent" and "nothing has been kept". %>
              <% if run.nil? %>
                <% if rejections.refusing? %>
                  <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :error) { "No runs accepted" } %>
                <% else %>
                  <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :neutral) { "No runs yet" } %>
                <% end %>
              <% else %>
                <% suite_size = run.total_specs_count.to_i %>
                <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :info) { "#{number_with_delimiter(suite_size)} #{'test'.pluralize(suite_size)}" } %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
            <%# THE REFUSAL MARKER — the verdict this grid could not reach until now, in the error
                tone the connection indicator gives it and for the same stated reason: work is being
                destroyed, not merely absent.

                Both surfaces word it through `refused_deliveries_label` / `refused_deliveries_note`
                in ApplicationHelper, so this card and the page it links to cannot describe one
                repository's refusal two ways, and the next rename of either cannot move one surface
                without the other. That is the rule `test_run_delivery_note` and `test_run_cost_rows`
                below already hold this same card to. The seams own the WORDING; the containers
                differ — a header row there, a badge row and a paragraph here — so the markup stays
                at each caller.

                Rendered ABOVE the basis paragraph rather than inside it, because that paragraph is
                gated on `run` and the never-accepted case has no run at all — the state that most
                needs the sentence is exactly the one that would not have printed it. No link to the
                rejection panel: the whole card already links to the page that holds it. %>
            <% if rejections.refusing? %>
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
                <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :error) { refused_deliveries_label } %>
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-app-muted"><%= refused_deliveries_note(rejections.last_rejection_at) %></p>
            <% end %>
            <%# The badge's basis, on the surface beside it — the rule `show` and the API already
                hold this column to, and this grid needs it more than either of them does, because
                it renders N repositories at once and a row of badges is a comparison by
                construction.

                Two readings are reachable without it, and neither is exotic.
                `Repository#latest_test_run` returns the newest row whatever its age, so a
                repository whose CI broke five months ago prints its figure in identical type,
                tone and words to one that reported an hour ago — the age is the only thing that
                separates them. And on a sharded run `total_specs_count` is the SUM over the shards
                recorded SO FAR, while `created_at` is stamped by the FIRST shard's POST, so a
                four-shard suite mid-build sits in the grid at a quarter size with nothing marking
                it, and a job cancelled at shard two leaves that row there forever.

                Rendered only where there is a run: the "No runs yet" card has no basis to state,
                and a "never" beside it would be a second rendering of the same absence. %>
            <% if run %>
              <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
                <%# `branch` is nullable (db/schema.rb) and Ingest::Payload accepts a body without
                    it, so a silently shortened sentence would be a client omission dressed up as a
                    fact. Same words the Recent-runs table on `show` gives an absent branch. %>
                Ingested <%= time_ago_in_words(run.created_at) %> ago<%= run.branch.present? ? " on #{run.branch}" : ", branch not reported" %>.
                <%# What the figure above covers, when it does not cover the whole suite. Through
                    the same `test_run_delivery_note` seam the Recent-runs table renders, so this
                    card cannot word the fact differently from the page it links to — and
                    unconditional there, per-row, but only on a run that arrived in parts here: a
                    grid of cards each repeating "reported in one piece" would bury the one card
                    that is not. That is a reason to exclude ZERO shards, and `positive?` excludes
                    exactly that. A ONE-shard card is the case the sentence is most needed on —
                    a build still arriving, printing a fraction of its suite in the same type as
                    a whole one. %>
                <% if run.shard_count.positive? %>
                  <span class="block"><%= test_run_delivery_note(run) %></span>
                <% end %>
              </p>
              <%# What that run COST, beside what it contains. The badge above says how BIG each
                  suite is; nothing on this grid said what any of them is worth in CI minutes, which
                  is the question a page rendering N repositories at once exists to answer — the
                  reader with eight cards wants to know which one is burning their budget, and no
                  amount of suite size answers it.

                  THE SAME SEAM `show` RENDERS — `test_run_cost_rows` owns the labels, the pairing
                  with their denominators and the one-row/two-row branch, and both surfaces call it,
                  so a card CANNOT word a figure differently from the page it links to. That is the
                  rule the `test_run_delivery_note` above is already held to, and it is a property of
                  the code rather than of two sets of literals that happen to agree: re-spelling the
                  labels here would leave the next rename free to move one surface and not the other,
                  with nothing in the suite able to see it. The figures come with their treatment —
                  an absent one is styled and worded as absent, and a PARTIAL machine time is
                  deliberately not muted, because "at least 3m 15s" is a real measurement whose
                  incompleteness is carried by the wording.

                  The card passes no figures, so it gets the bare seams; `show` passes its
                  delta-decorated ones. The two surfaces differ in what they DECORATE and cannot
                  differ in what they SAY.

                  Free of queries. `duration_seconds` is a column on the row
                  `RepositoriesController#latest_test_runs` already selected, and every shard fact
                  the seam reads — the count, the timed count, the SUM — is primed for the whole grid
                  by the one grouped aggregate that method takes (`ShardCountPreloading`).
                  `spec/requests/repositories_spec.rb` pins that at one `test_run_shards` SELECT for
                  a grid of four four-shard cards, and the same one when the grid is doubled to
                  eight. %>
              <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: test_run_cost_rows(run)) %>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
          title: "No repositories yet",
          description: "Register a GitHub repository to start collecting spec intents from CI."
        ) do |empty| %>
      <% empty.with_action do %>
        <%# Both entry points here too — a first-time visitor who has just installed the App across
            an account is exactly the reader with twenty repositories to register, and this empty
            state is the one place they are guaranteed to see. %>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-2">
          <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :primary, href: new_repository_path) { "Register a repository" } %>
          <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :secondary, href: bulk_repositories_path) { "Register several" } %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
